--- title Collecting while parsing
--- about COLLECT and KEEP gather values as a rule walks. The part worth
--- about pinning is that collecting does not backtrack when matching
--- about does: a KEEP that ran inside a branch that then failed still
--- about leaves its value in the answer. Nothing about the rest of PARSE
--- about suggests that, so every entry here was asked of R3 3.22.1.

--- id parse-collect/an-empty-collect-answers-an-empty-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not none, so a caller can append to the answer without testing
--- note it first.
--- code
parse [] [collect []]
--- result
[]

--- id parse-collect/leftover-input-stops-mattering
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Ordinarily leftover input fails a parse. With COLLECT the
--- note question is no longer whether the rule accounted for all of it.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep integer!]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse-collect/one-match-is-kept-as-itself
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1] [collect [keep skip]]
--- result
[1]

--- id parse-collect/several-matches-are-kept-as-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note So a caller can tell one run from two. The count decides the
--- note shape, not the kind of rule.
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [collect [keep integer! keep 2 integer!]]
--- result
[1 [2 3]]

--- id parse-collect/keep-pick-keeps-them-separately
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect some [keep pick 2 integer!]]
--- result
[1 2]

--- id parse-collect/a-failed-branch-still-leaves-what-it-kept
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The whole point of this file. The first branch keeps the 1 and
--- note then fails on the string; the alternative keeps the same 1
--- note again. Both are in the answer, so the 1 appears twice.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [keep integer! keep string! | keep integer!]]
--- result
[1 1]

--- id parse-collect/the-same-through-nested-blocks
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect [[keep integer! keep string!] | [keep integer!]]]
--- result
[1 1]

--- id parse-collect/a-nested-collect-leaves-a-block-even-when-it-fails
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The inner collect hands its block to the collection around it
--- note when it starts, not when it succeeds. Its rule needs two items
--- note and there are none, and the empty block is in the answer.
--- code
parse [] [collect [collect [keep 2 skip]]]
--- result
[[]]

--- id parse-collect/a-nested-collect-keeps-what-it-got-before-failing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The inner rule keeps the 1, then wants a second integer and
--- note there is none. The 1 stays.
--- code
parse [1] [collect some [collect [keep integer! keep integer!]]]
--- result
[[1]]

--- id parse-collect/a-partial-last-round-shows-up-as-its-own-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1 2 3] [collect some [collect [keep integer! keep integer!]]]
--- result
[[1 2] [3]]

--- id parse-collect/some-tries-one-more-time-than-it-succeeds
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is why there is a trailing empty block: SOME attempts a
--- note third round, the inner COLLECT starts and contributes its
--- note block, and only then does the KEEP fail.
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect some [collect keep integer!]]
--- result
[[1] [2] []]

--- id parse-collect/the-trailing-block-appears-with-a-following-keep-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect some [collect keep integer! keep ('a)]]
--- result
[[1] a [2] a []]

--- id parse-collect/and-when-the-inner-rule-is-a-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1 2] [collect some [collect [keep integer! keep ('a)]]]
--- result
[[1 a] [2 a] []]

--- id parse-collect/a-collect-answers-its-block-even-when-nothing-matched
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [] [collect [keep 2 skip]]
--- result
[]

--- id parse-collect/collects-nest-three-deep
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [] [collect [collect [collect []]]]
--- result
[[[]]]

--- id parse-collect/an-outer-failure-does-not-discard-the-inner-block
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The inner collect keeps the 1; the outer KEEP then runs out of
--- note input. The answer still holds the inner block.
--- code
parse [1] [collect [collect [keep skip] keep skip]]
--- result
[[1]]

--- id parse-collect/two-nested-collects-side-by-side
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse [1] [collect [collect [keep skip] collect [keep skip]]]
--- result
[[1] []]

--- id parse-collect/collecting-from-a-string-at-all
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note COLLECT works the same way on a string parse as on a block one,
--- note which is worth pinning because the two are separate walks and
--- note only one of them had it.
--- code
parse "" [collect []]
--- result
[]

--- id parse-collect/leftover-string-input-stops-mattering-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "a" [collect []]
--- result
[]

--- id parse-collect/one-character-is-kept-as-a-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not as a one-character string. This is the string parse's
--- note version of "one match is kept as itself".
--- code
parse "a" [collect [keep skip]]
--- result
[#"a"]

--- id parse-collect/each-round-keeps-its-own-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "abc" [collect any [keep skip]]
--- result
[#"a" #"b" #"c"]

--- id parse-collect/several-characters-are-kept-as-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And the boundary between the two shapes shows up in one
--- note answer: "ab" is a string and the leftover c is a character.
--- code
parse "abc" [collect any [keep 1 2 skip]]
--- result
["ab" #"c"]

--- id parse-collect/what-is-kept-keeps-the-inputs-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Parsing a file gives file pieces, not string pieces. A run of
--- note characters is a slice of the input rather than a fresh string.
--- code
parse %abc [collect any [keep 1 2 skip]]
--- result
[%ab #"c"]

--- id parse-collect/a-run-matched-by-some-is-one-piece
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "aabbb" [collect [keep some "a" keep some #"b"]]
--- result
["aa" "bbb"]

--- id parse-collect/a-count-keeps-one-piece
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "ab" [collect [keep 2 skip]]
--- result
["ab"]

--- id parse-collect/keep-pick-keeps-the-characters-separately
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the whole difference PICK makes: same rule, two
--- note characters instead of one string.
--- code
parse "ab" [collect [keep pick 2 skip]]
--- result
[#"a" #"b"]

--- id parse-collect/keeping-what-to-skipped-over
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "abc" [collect [keep to "c"]]
--- result
["ab"]

--- id parse-collect/keeping-what-thru-passed
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "abc" [collect [keep thru "b"]]
--- result
["ab"]

--- id parse-collect/a-paren-keeps-its-value-in-a-string-parse
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
parse "a" [collect [keep ("x")]]
--- result
["x"]
